#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060874 :tutorial/content "Let's consider an equilateral triangle with sides of length $2x$. Notice that any altitude will split it into two congruent triangles, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nIn the new smaller triangle the acute angles will be $30^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ$. Such triangles are called **$\\boldsymbol{30^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{60^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{90^\\circ}$ triangles**.\n\nBased on the picture above, we see a special property shared by all $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles:\n\n> *In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nThis also works in the other direction:\n\n> *If the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg, then the given right triangle is a ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "e07c8310-fe8a-5f3e-9ff0-7cd4e93a784c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2383 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060875 :tutorial/content "Consider the same ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle from before. This time, however, we will focus on the height ($h$) instead of the hypotenuse.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nApplying the Pythagorean Theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x)^{2} &= x^{2} + h^{2} \\\\\nh^{2} &= 4x^{2} - x^{2} \\\\\nh^{2} &= 3x^{2} \\\\\nh &= \\sqrt{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the second important property:\n\n> *In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg (which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle) is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the length of the shorter leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nWe can summarize everything we know about ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangles using the diagram below:\n\n![](images/85/8598f9163b29833bb5895416e3dec9b6aa2358bd2cfcca944625ebb9f576b3a4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2dbb6c4-dedc-56fa-8e66-87e843a7f7fb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2386 :tutorial/title "The Longer Leg"}]}
